= Solution
{parent=ii}

Six neutral <ammonia> donors leave Cu(II), $d^9$, so
$$\boxed{N_e=9+6(2)=21.}$$
The donor skeleton has **<octahedral molecular geometry> with a pronounced tetragonal Jahn–Teller distortion**, normally four shorter equatorial and two longer axial Cu–N distances. The $t_{2g}^6e_g^3$ occupation is orbitally degenerate in the ideal octahedral limit. An <electron> count above 18 is not a contradiction: <ammonia> is primarily a sigma donor and the occupied metal-derived antibonding levels make this a weak-field coordination complex rather than a low-valent carbonyl closed shell.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Neutral CO ligands require formal Cr($-\mathrm{II}$), hence $d^8$. With five two-electron <carbonyl ligands>,
$$\boxed{N_e=8+5(2)=18.}$$
A **<trigonal-bipyramidal molecular geometry>** is expected, analogous in <electron> count to $\mathrm{Fe(CO)_5}$. Strong <pi-acceptor ligands> stabilize a low <oxidation state> through backbonding, and this five-coordinate structure accommodates the closed-shell 18-electron configuration. The negative <oxidation state> is a formal assignment; it does not mean that a free <chromium> dianion is trapped inside the molecule.

= iv
{parent=a}
